cy trade  policy etc have benefited asset owners  at the expense of wage earners and so I  think we're seeing  assets expanding wages being stagnant  for almost 30 years and but it was  really only ten years ago that you know  the median income was 85 in the median  wealth down and this concentration at  the top keeps - just continues to defy  gravity really and when we think about  you know one element we've talked about  is productivity for the average worker  continues to increase in other words the  average worker is producing whether it  be goods or services more than ever  before but as you mentioned their wage  is not going up and that talked to us a  little bit about that right I mean the  additional productivity that workers are  creating is sort of being moved up in a  redistribution of income as some like to  say to do those at the top of the income  brackets and big corporation yeah so I  would say a huge driver is you know  productivity has gone up wages have  stayed low most of that productivity  gain then the wealth and income gains  from that have gone to not just really  the top 1% but what we're seeing really  in the last 8 or 9 years is it's going  to the top one-tenth of 1% who captured  most of the income and wealth gains at  the top so that's clearly a driver and  you know as the French economist Thomas  Piketty has pointed out if you just sort  of if that's the machinery if left  without intervening in any what way  where we're heading is we're seeing  going to a kind of a concentrated wealth  of a sort of a plutocracy where wealth  continues to concentrate wealth and  power continue to concentrate at the top  so we have to do something to intervene  in that inequality cycle so people can  have different opinions as to whether  this is moral or ethical what we see  happening but let's not have that  conversation let's have the conversation  as to what is the economic impact of  such wealth inequality and then whether  it's moral or not becomes sort of  irrelevant if we can make some general  assessments as to whether this is a good  or bad thing for the economy at large so  how can we think about that well one  thing thing is that this concept  wealth actually kind of creates a huge  amount of distortions and and volatility  in the economy so you have real wages  staying flat or falling for most of the  population that means that the aggregate  demand the consumer buying demand of  people at the bottom in the middle is  really not as engaged and then at the  very top you have this unreal  concentration of assets which leads to  sort of some unreal risk-taking meaning  people at the very top I'm talking about  you know the top 1% of households are  starting to move and a portion of their  wealth into more financially speculative  investments investments that get higher  returns than the real economy which  isn't you know returning a lot in terms  of interest or earnings so you have this  twin dynamic you have stagnant wages on  the bottom and then you have a  concentrated wealth at the top looking  more and more venturing into the sort of  speculative arena of the marketplace and  all of that leads to certain volatility  huge volatility so just take the energy  market today or over the last couple of  months you know or real estate in major  urban areas those are all local and  national and global economies that are  that are being distorted by these great  inequities of wealth and as we also know  that it sort of undermines the  traditional measures of growth I mean  the International Monetary Fund is now  showing up at the global level how these  inequalities actually undermine  traditional measures of economic growth  so too much too much inequality is bad  for the economy in a number of ways and  what about the social impact you know  the economic analysis you've done is  very clear we've talked about it before  we've spoken to other economists about  it what about the sort of intangible  social impact of such wealth inequality  well I think what we know is that as  societies become more an equal there is  a breakdown in solidarity where people  no longer feel that they're in the same  boat and it actually increases sort of a  sense of fear and polarization I would  argue in some ways our 2016 presidential  election dynamics where you have a  annex Anders candidate they're both  emerging out of the same phenomena  witches this is what happens when middle  classes collapse when people feel like  the economy is is rigged against them  whatever political analysis they take  with that may send them at different  directions but the underlying problem is  this polarization so I think it leads to  kind of a whole bunch of negative  indicators it's bad for health it's bad  for public health even for very wealthy  people it's bad for social cohesion  it's undermines sort of neighborhoods  when and communities when they're these  volatile and destructive inequities so I  think it sort of pulls in our culture it  moves us in the wrong direction  and in terms of us as a society there's  certain issues we talk about where there  are very clear sort of action items or  asks that the average individual can do  in terms of engaging lawmakers or  engaging with the political system or  society etc with this one it's been more  difficult for us on the program to  figure out what individuals can do I  mean certainly it would be great to  elect more politicians who would put in  policies that would have a sort of  equalizing effect to reduce this drastic  income gap that we have but what can the  average person really do well I think  continuing to talk to their friends and  neighbors about why these inequalities  matter believe it or not is very  important because I think as a society  we've gone from ten years ago and we  really didn't talk about it to a broader  recognition and connect the dots these  inequalities really do affect the  quality of life for all of us then I  think 